<sec id="s1"><title>Introduction</title>
<p>South Asian adults develop type 2 diabetes at lower body mass index <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b1">[1]</xref>.</p>
<p>Dietary glycaemic index may matter.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="s2" sec-type="methods"><title>Materials and methods</title>
<sec id="s2a"><title>Participants</title><p>We recruited 240 adults with prediabetes in Chennai.</p></sec>
<sec id="s2b"><title>Intervention</title><p>Participants received a low glycaemic index meal plan.</p>
<table-wrap id="t1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Baseline characteristics</p></caption>
<table><thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Intervention</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>Age, years</td><td>45.2</td><td>44.8</td></tr><tr><td>BMI, kg/m2</td><td>26.1</td><td>25.9</td></tr></tbody></table>
<table-wrap-foot><p>Values are means.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</sec>
</sec>
<sec id="s3"><title>Results</title><p>Diabetes incidence was 12% versus 20% (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="t2">Table 2</xref>).</p>
<fig id="f1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Flow diagram caption should be skipped.</p></caption></fig>
</sec>
<sec id="s4"><title>Discussion</title><p>The effect is consistent with prior trials.</p></sec>
<sec id="s5"><title>Conclusions</title><p>A low GI diet reduced progression.</p></sec>
<sec id="s6"><title>Funding</title><p>Funded by a test grant.</p></sec>
